.Dd April 27, 2026
.Dt RE 4D
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm re
.Nd Realtek RTL8125/RTL8126/RTL8127 Ethernet Driver
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/net/re*
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
driver is a GLDv3 NIC driver for modern Realtek PCIe Ethernet
controllers.
Supported controller families include:
.Bl -bullet
.It
RTL8125 devices supporting speeds up to 2.5 Gbit/s
.It
RTL8126 devices supporting speeds up to 5 Gbit/s
.It
RTL8127 devices supporting speeds up to 10 Gbit/s
.El
.Pp
The
.Nm
driver supports checksum offload, MSI-X and MSI interrupts, multiple
receive and transmit queues on supported hardware, RSS receive
distribution, link state reporting, MAC properties, configurable
advertised link capabilities and temperature monitoring on supported
revisions.
.Pp
The driver is intended for RTL8125-class and newer devices.
Older RTL8169/RTL8111 families are outside the intended support scope.
.Pp
For each device supported by the
.Nm
driver, a character special file is created under
.Pa /dev/net .
The first instance has id 0 and is named
.Sy re0 ,
available at
.Pa /dev/net/re0 .
The device supports the Data Link Provider Interface
.Pq DLPI ,
documented in
.Xr dlpi 4P
and commonly accessed through
.Xr libdlpi 3LIB .
.Sh LINK CONFIGURATION
The
.Nm
driver supports link mode configuration through
.Xr dladm 8 .
Supported properties include auto-negotiation, advertised link
capabilities and pause frame configuration.
.Pp
The exact set of available speeds depends on the controller revision and
link partner capability.
RTL8125 devices support speeds up to 2.5 Gbit/s, RTL8126 devices support
speeds up to 5 Gbit/s, and RTL8127 devices support speeds up to 10 Gbit/s.
.Pp
Manual speed selection is implemented by configuring the advertised PHY
capabilities.
The driver keeps PHY autonegotiation enabled and restarts
autonegotiation after advertised capabilities are changed.
.Sh INTERRUPTS
The
.Nm
driver supports MSI-X and MSI interrupt delivery.
MSI-X is preferred when available because it allows queue-specific
interrupt handling and improved scalability.
MSI is used as a single-vector fallback mode.
.Pp
On supported systems the driver may use multiple receive and transmit
queues when sufficient MSI-X resources are available.
.Pp
If the platform cannot provide the required MSI-X vectors, the driver
automatically falls back to MSI mode.
.Sh TIMER INTERRUPT MODE
The
.Nm
driver provides an optional timer interrupt mode configured through
.Pa re.conf .
This mode is intended as a compatibility and stability option for systems
where direct interrupt completion handling is unreliable.
.Pp
The recommended vendor default timer value is:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
timer_count=9728;
.Ed
.Pp
This value is equivalent to:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
0x2600
.Ed
.Pp
When used in MSI mode, timer interrupt mode causes the controller to
generate interrupts continuously at the configured timer rate,
independent of traffic load.
.Pp
For example, on RTL8125B with the default
.Sy timer_count=9728 ,
the interrupt rate is approximately 8700 interrupts per second.
.Pp
This mode may improve compatibility on systems affected by missed
interrupts or unstable completion handling, at the cost of increased CPU
interrupt load.
.Pp
When MSI-X mode is active, enabling timer interrupts enables hardware
interrupt mitigation.
Under high packet rates, multiple packet completions may be grouped into
fewer interrupts, reducing CPU overhead.
.Pp
The default configuration should be used unless system-specific behavior
requires tuning.
.Sh TEMPERATURE MONITORING
Selected controllers provide an internal temperature sensor exposed
through the illumos Fault Management Architecture
.Pq FMA
topology framework.
.Pp
Temperature monitoring is supported on RTL8125B and newer revisions,
including supported RTL8126 and RTL8127 controllers.
.Pp
If no sensor is displayed, the installed controller revision may not
provide a readable temperature sensor or support may be unavailable for
that hardware revision.
.Pp
Reported values represent controller internal temperature estimates and
are most useful for trend monitoring.
.Sh CONFIGURATION
Driver tunables may be placed in:
.Pa /kernel/drv/re.conf
.Pp
The following properties are supported:
.Bl -tag -width Sy
.It Sy force_msi
Select interrupt mode.
A value of 0 enables automatic selection.
A value of 1 forces MSI/MSI-X operation and disables legacy fallback.
.It Sy timer_int_enable
Enable timer interrupt mode.
A value of 0 disables the feature.
A value of 1 enables the feature.
.It Sy timer_count
Timer interrupt counter value.
The recommended vendor default is 9728.
.El
.Pp
The default configuration should be used unless the system exhibits
interrupt loss, TX/RX stalls or other platform-specific instability.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Display temperature sensor information for
.Sy re0 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
# /usr/lib/fm/fmd/fmtopo -V '*re0*'
.Ed
.Pp
Example
.Pa /kernel/drv/re.conf :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
#
# re.conf -- Realtek re driver tunables
#

force_msi=0;
timer_int_enable=0;
timer_count=9728;
.Ed
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Pa
.It Pa /kernel/drv/amd64/re
Device driver, x86 systems
.It Pa /kernel/drv/re.conf
Driver configuration file
.It Pa /dev/net/re*
Network device nodes
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr libdlpi 3LIB ,
.Xr dlpi 4P ,
.Xr dladm 8
.Sh NOTES
PCIe ASPM and power-management policy should normally be controlled by
system firmware.
Some controller revisions are sensitive to runtime PCIe power-management
changes.
If unexplained packet stalls, interrupt loss or link instability occur,
disable ASPM in BIOS or UEFI firmware before adding driver workarounds.
